WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.900
A sympathetic neuron loses its NGF.

2
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.700
What happens, step by step, before the killers switch on?

3
00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:11.990
Keep the worm in mind.

4
00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.250
There, EGL-1 takes the brake off the killers.

5
00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:20.950
The neuron's brake works one step earlier, as a gate at the mitochondria, which

6
00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:22.070
store cytochrome c.

7
00:00:23.083 --> 00:00:26.183
Step one. The survival signal falls.

8
00:00:27.217 --> 00:00:32.297
NGF from the target acts through its receptor, TrkA, on the axon terminals.

9
00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:36.147
The signal keeps kinases such as Akt active.

10
00:00:37.007 --> 00:00:38.987
Akt holds back several pro-death proteins.

11
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.630
Without NGF, that restraint weakens.

12
00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:46.750
Step two. Pro-death genes are switched on.

13
00:00:47.733 --> 00:00:49.453
A stress pathway turns on.

14
00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:52.193
The kinase JNK becomes active.

15
00:00:53.217 --> 00:00:57.297
JNK adds phosphates to a transcription factor called c-Jun.

16
00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:02.870
c-Jun rises in deprived neurons, and blocking its function protects them.

17
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:08.593
Among the genes then induced are BH3-only proteins, such as Bim.

18
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:13.933
This is part of the new gene expression that the 1988 drugs blocked.

19
00:01:14.933 --> 00:01:17.513
Step three. The gate opens.

20
00:01:18.583 --> 00:01:21.163
A BH3 domain is a short helix.

21
00:01:21.943 --> 00:01:25.403
It fits a groove on protective proteins, such as Bcl-2.

22
00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:29.343
That is what EGL-1 does to CED-9 in the worm.

23
00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:34.147
Protectors with filled grooves can no longer hold Bax back.

24
00:01:34.907 --> 00:01:36.767
Bim can also activate Bax directly.

25
00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:42.807
Inactive Bax sits in the cytoplasm, with its last helix tucked into its own groove.

26
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:46.467
Active Bax moves to the mitochondria.

27
00:01:47.087 --> 00:01:49.447
It inserts that helix into the outer membrane.

28
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:53.967
With other Bax molecules, it makes the membrane leaky.

29
00:01:54.527 --> 00:01:56.827
Cytochrome c escapes into the cytoplasm.

30
00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.610
Step four. The killers assemble.

31
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.500
Cytochrome c binds Apaf-1.

32
00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:07.400
Apaf-1 assembles into a wheel, called the apoptosome.

33
00:02:08.333 --> 00:02:11.203
The apoptosome activates caspase-9.

34
00:02:11.203 --> 00:02:14.353
Caspase-9 cuts and activates caspase-3.

35
00:02:15.333 --> 00:02:17.393
Caspase-3 takes the cell apart.

36
00:02:18.366 --> 00:02:21.006
Now delete Bax, and take NGF away.

37
00:02:21.906 --> 00:02:22.946
Which steps still happen?

38
00:02:23.866 --> 00:02:24.866
Does the neuron live?

39
00:02:31.116 --> 00:02:33.036
Steps one and two should still happen.

40
00:02:33.876 --> 00:02:35.056
They lie upstream of Bax.

41
00:02:36.066 --> 00:02:37.606
Step three cannot happen.

42
00:02:38.406 --> 00:02:41.286
The gate stays shut, and cytochrome c stays inside.

43
00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:43.670
The neuron survives.

44
00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:48.870
But it survives as a shrunken cell, because NGF also supports its growth.

45
00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:51.300
That is the first brake.

46
00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:53.340
Brake one is the gate.

47
00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.520
NGF loss opens it through new gene expression.
